 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Israel votes today, the first time since October seventh. Will anyone win?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Final polls on three Israeli TV channels put Gadi Eisenkot's Yashar party narrowly first. Twenty-two to twenty-three seats, against nineteen to twenty-one for Netanyahu's Likud. The Knesset has one hundred twenty seats. A government needs sixty-one."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "But those polls show no majority. All three give Netanyahu's bloc exactly fifty seats. The opposition bloc gets fifty-two to fifty-six. Both fall short of sixty-one. Small parties, including those backed by Palestinian citizens of Israel, could decide it."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "The issues are Iran, Gaza, and drafting ultra-Orthodox men. NPR reports Netanyahu suggests he wants to strike Iran again. Eisenkot's campaign pushes the draft. Analysts warn that coalition talks could take weeks, or end in another election."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will Israel have a new government by January? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
