 "upload": {
  "title": "Israel Votes: Can Anyone Win a Majority? #shorts",
  "description": "Israel holds a general election on Tuesday, October 27, 2026, the first since the October 7, 2023 attacks. Final polls on Israel's three main TV channels put Gadi Eisenkot's Yashar party narrowly ahead of Benjamin Netanyahu's Likud (22 to 23 seats against 19 to 21 in the 120-seat Knesset), but all three show Netanyahu's bloc at 50 seats and the opposition bloc at 52 to 56, short of the 61 needed for a government. Smaller parties, including those representing Palestinian citizens of Israel, could be decisive. Main issues: Iran, Gaza and military service for ultra-Orthodox men. NPR reports Netanyahu has suggested he wants to strike Iran again. Coalition talks could take weeks.\n\nSources:\nAnadolu Agency: Israel votes Tuesday in closely contested election as Netanyahu faces challenge from Eisenkot (Oct 2026)\nYnet News: Final Israel election polls put Eisenkot's Yashar ahead of Likud, but neither bloc wins a majority (Oct 2026)\nCNN: Israel is holding its most important election in years. Here's what's at stake (Oct 25, 2026)\nNPR: Israel holds consequential election after years of war (Oct 25, 2026)\nCNBC: Israel election 2026: war, security and coalition math (Oct 26, 2026)\nForeign Policy: Neither Netanyahu nor his opponents are expected to win outright (Oct 20, 2026)\n\n#geopolitics #Israel #Netanyahu #election",
